const MOVIES_CSM = [{"num":607,"t":"Modern Times","y":"1936","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Families will cherish Chaplin's silent slapstick,\" says CSM, though beneath the sight gags is a pointed Depression-era story of class struggle: a man dies in a workers' riot, prompting his daughter's grief, and Chaplin's Tramp unknowingly ingests a mysterious white powder and later gets drunk. Otherwise it's mostly gentle physical comedy. Positive: the Tramp meets hardship with kindness, resourcefulness, and unfailing optimism, and the gamine he befriends shows real cleverness and independence.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/modern-times","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Drama"],"studio":"United Artists","source":"CSM"},{"num":608,"t":"Bringing Up Baby","y":"1938","la":"6+","ca":"8+","full":"\"Classic screwball comedy with loads of tame laughs.\" The mayhem -- a wayward leopard named Baby, a family gardener who nips from a flask for laughs, and a string of pratfalls and mistaken identities -- never leads to real injury or real danger. Some flirtatious banter and a single quip about a character going \"gay\" reflect the era. Positive: Katharine Hepburn's Susan brings feisty, independent energy that puts her on equal footing with Cary Grant's uptight David, and the film's message is that spontaneity and joy are worth loosening up for.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/bringing-up-baby","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Romance"],"studio":"RKO Radio Pictures","source":"CSM"},{"num":609,"t":"An American in Paris","y":"1951","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"\"Spectacular musical falters in treatment of women.\" The plot leans hard into old-fashioned romance: characters kiss passionately, drink cocktails and champagne in nightclubs, and Jerry doggedly pursues Lise even as she keeps turning him away, a dynamic the film treats as swoon-worthy rather than a red flag. Some French dialogue goes without subtitles, and the story references WWII occupation and the Resistance. Positive: Gershwin's musical numbers and Gene Kelly's dancing celebrate art and beauty as healing forces, even if the central romance hasn't aged as well as the choreography.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/an-american-in-paris","genre":["Live-Action","Musical","Romance"],"studio":"MGM","source":"CSM"},{"num":610,"t":"Born Free","y":"1966","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Compelling classic nature story with some animal violence.\" This true story of Joy Adamson raising lioness Elsa includes real predation (lions killing and eating other animals), an offscreen-then-shown lion attack that leaves blood in a river, and a scary elephant stampede; adults drink at meals. Positive: Joy Adamson's tireless devotion to preparing Elsa for life in the wild models real stewardship of, and respect for, the natural world.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/born-free","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Drama"],"studio":"Other","source":"CSM"},{"num":611,"t":"The Jungle Book (1967)","y":"1967","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Jaunty animation, jazzy songs, iffy representation,\" per CSM. The Baloo/Shere Khan showdown includes a burning branch tied to the tiger's tail and Kaa's hypnotic near-miss, and CSM flags dated, stereotypical portrayals in King Louie and the monkeys' characterization. Positive: Mowgli's animal friends model courage and teamwork as they work together to keep him safe, wrapped in some of Disney's catchiest songs.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-jungle-book-1967","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy","Musical"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":612,"t":"Chitty Chitty Bang Bang","y":"1968","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"A fantastical car story custom-made for kids,\" says CSM. The cartoonish action (no one is actually hurt) is safe, but the sinister Child Catcher, who lures kids with candy before a long chase/battle, unsettles many young viewers, and a party scene includes drinking, smoking, and a lingerie dance number. Positive: Caractacus Potts is an inventive, devoted single father, and the film's message that ordinary people (and things) hold real worth resonates through Truly Scrumptious's kindness and the children's loyalty.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/chitty-chitty-bang-bang","genre":["Live-Action","Musical","Fantasy"],"studio":"MGM","source":"CSM"},{"num":613,"t":"Charlotte's Web (1973)","y":"1973","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Heartwarming tale of love and friendship.\" The animated musical doesn't shy from real themes of death and the cycle of life -- Wilbur faces the threat of slaughter, and Charlotte herself dies near the end, which can be unsettling for younger viewers. Positive: friendship and love sit at the center of the story, with the film modeling how to process loss and keep loved ones alive through memory.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/charlottes-web-1973","genre":["Animation","Musical","Drama"],"studio":"Paramount","source":"CSM"},{"num":614,"t":"Freaky Friday (1976)","y":"1976","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Endearing '76 original may feel outdated to kids.\" Mother and teen daughter Annabel swap bodies for a day and must survive each other's lives, leading to comic car chases and mishaps but nothing scary. There's a passing reference to a relative's struggle with alcohol (no drinking shown), and the underage Annabel ends up behind the wheel of a car for laughs. Positive: the swap builds real empathy between mother and daughter, showing how walking in someone else's shoes fosters mutual respect.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/freaky-friday-1976","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":615,"t":"Annie (1982)","y":"1982","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Classic orphan tale has great songs, some iffy content.\" Concerns: Annie dangles from a bridge while fleeing a kidnapper, a bomb is thrown into Daddy Warbucks' office, orphanage matron Miss Hannigan shoves the girls around and is frequently played-for-laughs drunk with a bottle in hand, and kids are called names like \"pig droppings.\" Positive: Annie's grit, determination, and optimism, and the theme that a loving family can be built on more than blood.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/annie-1982","genre":["Live-Action","Musical","Comedy"],"studio":"Other","source":"CSM"},{"num":616,"t":"E.T.: The Extra-Terrestrial","y":"1982","la":"6+","ca":"7+","full":"\"Spielberg's family classic is still one of the best.\" Government agents pursue E.T. through the story, there's a genuinely distressing sequence where he appears to be dying, and Elliott and E.T. share a comedic scene where both get accidentally drunk. Brief strong language includes \"s--t\" and \"son of a bitch,\" plus an outdated slur during a Peter Pan reading. Positive: Elliott's loyalty, courage, and empathy in protecting his otherworldly friend, and his mother's steady, caring presence as a single parent, anchor the film's themes of friendship and trust.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/et-the-extra-terrestrial","genre":["Live-Action","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Universal","source":"CSM"},{"num":617,"t":"An American Tail","y":"1986","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Heartwarming tale of immigrant mouse has peril,\" says CSM. Fievel's family flees pogroms in Russia and gets separated crossing to America, and the film doesn't shy from real danger: a village burns, cats stalk mice through the streets, and adults drink at a wake while a cigar gets passed around. Positive: Fievel's persistence and hope carry him through, his parents never give up searching for him, and a sympathetic cat proves that kindness crosses species (and cultural) lines.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/an-american-tail","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Drama"],"studio":"Universal","source":"CSM"},{"num":618,"t":"Anne of Green Gables","y":"1986","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"\"Faithful, sensitive take on classic novel is great for kids,\" per CSM. Content is mild for the age: Anne accidentally gets her best friend Diana tipsy by mixing up a bottle at tea, a beloved character's death and an infant's illness carry real emotional weight, and one teacher's attention to a teen student raises an eyebrow. Positive: Anne is imaginative, hardworking, and quick to make amends for her impulsive mistakes, while Marilla and Matthew show that found family can be as loving as any other kind.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/anne-of-green-gables","genre":["Live-Action","Drama","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"Sullivan Entertainment","source":"CSM"},{"num":619,"t":"The Adventures of Milo and Otis","y":"1986","la":"6+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Lovable pet tale about friendship despite differences,\" per CSM, though parents should know this is more intense than typical G-rated fare: the kitten and puppy stars encounter real predatory bears and seagulls, Milo jumps off a cliff, and the film has long faced unconfirmed animal-rights allegations about how some scenes were shot. Positive: Milo and Otis show unconditional loyalty and courage across their differences, modeling the idea that if they can be devoted friends, so can people.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-adventures-of-milo-and-otis","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Sony Pictures","source":"CSM"},{"num":620,"t":"The Brave Little Toaster","y":"1987","la":"7+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Appliances make a suspenseful, incredible journey,\" per CSM, but this one runs darker than expected: parents flag genuinely frightening scenes, including appliances dropped into a waterfall, sucked into quicksand, and chased by a scrapyard's malicious supermagnet, plus a subplot that reads as an appliance's near-suicidal sacrifice. No language or substance concerns. Positive: the toaster and friends model loyalty and teamwork under pressure, and the story gently critiques a throwaway culture that discards still-useful things for something newer.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-brave-little-toaster","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Fantasy"],"studio":"Hyperion Pictures","source":"CSM"},{"num":621,"t":"The Land Before Time","y":"1988","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Original baby-dinosaur buddy tale has scares, sadness,\" per CSM. Littlefoot's mother dies after a battle with a Sharptooth early on, and the young dinosaurs face earthquakes, volcanoes, and repeated chases from a hunting T. rex as they journey alone to the Great Valley. Positive: Littlefoot leads with courage and good judgment, timid Petrie overcomes his fears, and the group's growing tolerance across dinosaur \"kinds\" models teamwork and unselfishness.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-land-before-time","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Drama"],"studio":"Universal","source":"CSM"},{"num":622,"t":"Babar: The Movie","y":"1989","la":"6+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Animated elephant adventure has some peril.\" CSM warns of a brief but intense opening in which Babar's village is raided and burned, elephants are separated from their children and enslaved by the rhinos, and some are shown restrained and whipped. Positive: the film champions loyalty, standing by friends, and resolving conflict without violence, with Babar and his companions repaying kindness with good deeds throughout their journey to reclaim their home.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/babar-the-movie","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Musical"],"studio":"Nelvana","source":"CSM"},{"num":623,"t":"FernGully: The Last Rainforest","y":"1992","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Family favorite features strong environmental message.\" Fairy Crysta shrinks logger Zak down to size so he can experience the rainforest he's helping destroy, and together they face Hexxus, a menacing living blob of toxic sludge. The villain's attacks and a knife pulled out for a comedic beat bring some tension, plus one kiss and a \"bodacious babe\" comment. Positive: Crysta and the forest animals model kindness and care for nature, and Zak's arc teaches viewers to value and protect the environment.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ferngully-the-last-rainforest","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Musical"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"CSM"},{"num":624,"t":"The Muppet Christmas Carol","y":"1992","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"A gentle, fuzzy version of the Dickens classic,\" per CSM. The Ghost of Christmas Future is genuinely eerie for sensitive kids, and one vision shows Tiny Tim's projected death, though there's no real violence beyond Gonzo and Rizzo's slapstick tumbles. Positive: Scrooge's transformation from miser to generous, compassionate man -- learning to value relationships over wealth -- gives the film real heart alongside its Muppet humor and songs.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-muppet-christmas-carol","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Musical","Fantasy","Holiday"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":625,"t":"Heidi (1993)","y":"1993","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Family entertainment at its best,\" per CSM, though this nearly three-hour two-part TV movie earns its warning for emotional intensity: young Heidi is left with a grandfather who initially rejects her, and she later watches her ailing grandmother decline and die. No violence, language, or substance concerns. Positive: the film treats death honestly as a natural part of life rather than something to fear, and gruff Grandfather Alp's hidden warmth teaches that people aren't always what they seem on the surface.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/heidi-1993","genre":["Live-Action","Drama","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":626,"t":"The Nightmare Before Christmas","y":"1993","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"\"Tim Burton holiday magic, with a touch of scariness.\" Characters casually remove their own heads and limbs, skeletons and zombies populate Halloweentown, and the sinister Oogie Boogie menaces the cast; one gag involves a child unwrapping a decapitated head as a gift. Jack Skellington's scheme to kidnap Santa and take over Christmas drives real tension before he learns from his mistakes. Positive: Jack's arc rewards gratitude over restlessness, and Sally's willingness to speak up against his plan, even at personal risk, models quiet courage.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-nightmare-before-christmas","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Musical"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":627,"t":"The Thief and the Cobbler","y":"1993","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"A must for family viewing,\" per CSM, which lists no real concerns around violence, language, or romance, aside from noting the smallest children might be frightened by the hulking, menacing bad guys serving sorcerer Zigzag. Visually the film is a dazzling showcase of optical illusions from a Who Framed Roger Rabbit animator, with Vincent Price voicing the villain and Jonathan Winters stealing scenes as the thief. Positive: shy cobbler Tack proves his worth through courage and perseverance, and a musical number underscores that education and cleverness matter.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-thief-and-the-cobbler","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Adventure","Musical"],"studio":"MGM","source":"CSM"},{"num":628,"t":"Black Beauty","y":"1994","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"\"A harrowing and Beauty-ful version of the book,\" says CSM. Told from the horse's own point of view, the film doesn't soften the cruelty Black Beauty and his friend Ginger endure under bad owners; Ginger's fate, in particular, is only implied but lands hard on kids who've grown attached to her. No language, substance, or romantic content. Positive: the story invites real reflection on how animals deserve to be treated, and Black Beauty's endurance through both mistreatment and kindness models resilience.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/black-beauty","genre":["Live-Action","Drama","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"CSM"},{"num":629,"t":"The Lion King (1994)","y":"1994","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Musical king-of-the-beasts blockbuster is powerful, scary,\" says CSM. The wildebeest stampede that kills Mufasa, and Simba's discovery of his father's body, hit hard, and the militaristic hyenas and talk of eating rivals push this darker than typical Disney fare; mild insults like \"stupid\" and \"idiot\" round out the language. Positive: Simba grows from guilt and avoidance into a leader who faces his problems head-on, and Mufasa and Sarabi model selfless, protective love.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-lion-king-1994","genre":["Animation","Musical","Adventure"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":630,"t":"The Swan Princess","y":"1994","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Classic story is less scary than most animated fairy tales,\" per CSM. An evil sorcerer turns the princess into a swan, and there's a brief fire-and-lightning sequence where a beast is captured plus an arrow piercing a puffin's wing, but CSM notes these moments are quick and don't show real injury. Positive: the film's message is that loyalty, kindness, honesty, and cleverness matter more than looks -- both the princess and prince prove courageous and steadfast, and the prince learns not to judge by appearance alone.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-swan-princess","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Musical","Romance"],"studio":"Sony Pictures","source":"CSM"},{"num":631,"t":"Thumbelina","y":"1994","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Magical, musical story is a classic fairy tale.\" CSM flags a sword fight, a kidnapping-and-forced-marriage plot line, and a Beetle who won't stop calling Thumbelina \"Toots\" despite her objections; grown-ups sip cocktails at the Beetle Ball. Positive: strong messages about believing in yourself and not compromising your happiness just to please others.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/thumbelina","genre":["Animation","Musical","Fantasy"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"CSM"},{"num":632,"t":"A Goofy Movie","y":"1995","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Even tweens will enjoy this road trip with Goofy.\" Concerns include a scary car-off-a-cliff-into-a-river sequence, Max punching a mascot, Pete drinking beer and spitting it at the TV, and mild language (\"butt,\" an interrupted \"hell\"); a few background gags show women in slinky outfits. Positive: Goofy is an earnest, loving father learning patience as his son goes through an awkward, distancing phase, and their bond proves stronger than the friction.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/a-goofy-movie","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Musical"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":633,"t":"All Dogs Go to Heaven 2","y":"1996","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"This dark coming-of-age story is a unique romp.\" CSM notes cartoonish, mostly implied mayhem, references to Hell and the devious devil-figure Red, and mild innuendo that may unsettle younger or Christian-leaning families. Positive: Charlie's arc from selfish schemer to someone who works for others' good delivers a real lesson about acceptance and teamwork over self-interest.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/all-dogs-go-to-heaven-2","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Fantasy"],"studio":"MGM","source":"CSM"},{"num":634,"t":"Fly Away Home","y":"1996","la":"7+","ca":"8+","full":"\"Thrilling, touching adventure for animal lovers.\" The film opens with a jarring car accident that kills Amy's mother, shown from inside the flipping vehicle, and later includes hunters shooting at geese and one use of \"s--t.\" Adults drink wine and beer, and there's a brief shower scene with implied but not shown nudity. Positive: Amy and her father Tom's shared project to save an orphaned flock of Canada geese models perseverance, teamwork, and how a strained parent-child relationship can heal through shared purpose.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/fly-away-home","genre":["Live-Action","Adventure","Drama"],"studio":"Sony Pictures","source":"CSM"},{"num":635,"t":"Anastasia","y":"1997","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Beautiful, not factual depiction of missing Russian duchess.\" Concerns: Rasputin appears as a decaying, rotting corpse, and the film builds to a burning castle, a runaway train racing a collapsing bridge, and a character nearly swept overboard in a storm; a young couple kisses at the end. Positive: Anastasia is, per CSM, \"a thoroughly modern, courageous role model for girls\" whose honesty and perseverance carry her through danger she largely handles herself.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/anastasia","genre":["Animation","Musical","Adventure"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"CSM"},{"num":636,"t":"Cinderella (1997)","y":"1997","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Musical take on classic fairy tale is an inclusion landmark.\" There's comical slapstick, bullying and name-calling that could read as ableist (\"stupid,\" \"lazy\"), champagne bottles played for comedic drunkenness in a musical number, and only the mildest kissing-based romance. Positive: the story insists that money and status never determine worth -- courage, perseverance, humility, and kindness do -- and it's celebrated for its groundbreaking multiracial casting, with Brandy as Cinderella and Whitney Houston as her fairy godmother.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/cinderella-1997","genre":["Live-Action","Musical","Fantasy"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":637,"t":"Kirikou and the Sorceress","y":"1998","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Enchanting folk tale with toddler hero, for all ages.\" A snarling skunk chases the toddler hero, a warthog threatens him, and the evil sorceress Karaba holds the whole village hostage with magic and fear; there's mild name-calling (\"little toad\") and non-sexualized nudity throughout, including bare-chested women and brief, non-detailed views of the young protagonist. Positive: rather than seeking revenge, Kirikou tries to understand Karaba's motives and wins through loyalty, bravery, wisdom, and compassion, and the film offers a rich introduction to West African art, music, and culture.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/kirikou-and-the-sorceress","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Les Armateurs","source":"CSM"},{"num":638,"t":"Annie (1999)","y":"1999","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Joyous '90s version of classic musical,\" per CSM, this made-for-TV take on the Broadway musical has Miss Hannigan's cruelty played mostly for laughs, though her brother's threat to Annie with a knife and orphans plotting to spike her drink land as more menacing than musical-comedy filler. Positive: Annie stays defiantly optimistic and stands up to bullies despite a rough orphanage, and Grace models genuine warmth and advocacy for a kid who has none.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/annie-1999","genre":["Live-Action","Musical","Comedy"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":639,"t":"Babar: King of the Elephants","y":"1999","la":"6+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Delightful story, with one scary moment for younger kids.\" CSM flags an early scene where Babar's mother is shot and killed by a poacher, plus talk of war between the elephants and rhinos. Positive: Madame's kindness in taking in and educating orphaned Babar, and Babar's own patient, respectful leadership once he becomes king, model cooperation and resolving conflict through dialogue rather than violence.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/babar-king-of-the-elephants","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Drama"],"studio":"Nelvana","source":"CSM"},{"num":640,"t":"Muppets from Space","y":"1999","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Plenty of jokes and mayhem to keep kids and parents amused,\" says CSM. Gonzo is captured by a shady government agent who wants to study his brain, and there's frequent cartoon-style peril -- electrification, an explosion, a rescue mission -- all played for laughs rather than real scares; language stays mild (\"wacko,\" \"freak-boy\"). Positive: the story champions individuality and finding your place, with Kermit, Piggy, and the gang showing loyalty and teamwork to save their friend.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/muppets-from-space","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Adventure","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Sony Pictures","source":"CSM"},{"num":641,"t":"Stuart Little","y":"1999","la":"5+","ca":"7+","full":"\"Sweet book-based tale has some emotional intensity.\" Stuart faces real peril from the family cat and two conniving alley mice, including a scary moment stuck in a washing machine, alongside mild language like \"damn\" and \"hell\" and some cat-flatulence humor. The adoption storyline -- a human couple choosing a mouse as their son -- may land differently for adoptive or foster families. Positive: the Little family's patience and unconditional love model what makes a family, and the story champions kindness toward those who look different.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/stuart-little","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Comedy"],"studio":"Sony Pictures","source":"CSM"},{"num":642,"t":"Alvin and the Chipmunks Meet the Wolfman","y":"2000","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"A loveable horror story with life lessons.\" CSM flags scary nighttime encounters, an offscreen werewolf bite, sibling scuffles with visible scratches, and a chemistry mishap that causes an explosion. Positive: the brothers stick together despite their squabbles, a supportive principal helps a struggling student, and the story nudges viewers to understand a bully's motivations rather than just fear him.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/alvin-and-the-chipmunks-meet-the-wolfman","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Horror"],"studio":"Universal","source":"CSM"},{"num":643,"t":"An American Tail: The Mystery of the Night Monster","y":"2000","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"A mystery with spunk, courage, and heart.\" CSM warns of nightmare-style imagery, including a devil-bat with a mousetrap tongue, and a villain who steals Fievel's family and burns their home. Positive: Fievel pushes through his fears to help save the day, and the story frames fear as a natural protective instinct while showing that imagination can sometimes make danger seem bigger than it is.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/an-american-tail-the-mystery-of-the-night-monster","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Mystery"],"studio":"Universal","source":"CSM"},{"num":644,"t":"Buzz Lightyear of Star Command: The Adventure Begins","y":"2000","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"CSM describes it as \"a spirited romp through space.\" Cartoonish space battles bring explosions, a shattered robot, and Buzz's mechanical arm destroyed, and Buzz believes his partner has died and mourns him at a funeral, while villain Zurg uses a mind-control device called the Uni-Mind to brainwash a ranger. Positive: the film emphasizes teamwork, following the rules, and the value of having a trusted partner, even if some characters break their own rules along the way.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/buzz-lightyear-of-star-command-the-adventure-begins","genre":["Animation","Sci-Fi","Adventure"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":645,"t":"Ice Age","y":"2002","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Clever, funny, touching; like a great road movie.\" The film opens with a mother drowning while saving her infant, and saber-tooth tigers and rampaging rhinos attack in intense close-up sequences, with one character impaled on icicles. Despite the prehistoric setting, animals are often played as calculating villains rather than acting from instinct. Positive: Manny, Sid, and Diego's mismatched herd learns to sacrifice for one another across species lines, and even the story's antagonists get a chance to find respect and empathy.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ice-age","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Blue Sky Studios","source":"CSM"},{"num":646,"t":"Robots","y":"2005","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Endearing 'follow your dreams' story with plenty of laughs,\" per CSM. Robots face cartoon chases, falls, and captures, with the villain melting outdated robots down in a fiery \"chop shop\" oven; humor leans on a stretch of fart jokes and a party scene where robots sip \"oil\" from martini glasses. Positive: hero Rodney Copperbottom shows intelligence, loyalty, and perseverance, and the film's core message -- that you can shine no matter what you're made of -- champions acceptance over forced upgrades.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/robots","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"CSM"},{"num":647,"t":"Brother Bear 2","y":"2006","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"CSM calls it \"Bear's warm-furry quest finds love and friends.\" Kenai reunites with his childhood sweetheart Nita while the moose brothers crack crude jokes about female body parts, an avalanche and a pack of menacing raccoons create real peril, and a beloved character appears fatally injured before the story resolves. Positive: the film centers on friendship, love, and courage, with the theme that once you love someone, \"they stay in your heart forever.\"","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/brother-bear-2","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Musical"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":648,"t":"Charlotte's Web (2006)","y":"2006","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"CSM calls this version \"an enchanting take on a beloved children's classic.\" Concerns are mild: minor slapstick, crows chasing a rat, references to Wilbur's fate as Christmas dinner, occasional name-calling (\"stupid,\" \"hideous,\" \"bloody\"), and Charlotte's peaceful death followed by her friends' mourning. Positive: the film honors E.B. White's themes of friendship, loyalty, and acceptance -- Charlotte shows determination and generosity, Wilbur grows into courage and humility, Fern stays open-minded, and even Templeton the rat reveals hidden depth.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/charlottes-web-2006","genre":["Live-Action","Drama","Comedy"],"studio":"Paramount","source":"CSM"},{"num":649,"t":"Leroy & Stitch","y":"2006","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"More lively fun with Lilo, Stitch, and friends,\" per CSM. Villain Leroy has a devilish look and is cloned into a full army of himself, which could unsettle younger viewers, and there's a mild cartoon battle finale, but nothing graphic. Positive: previously bad experiments turn good and use their powers to help, wrapped in the Hawaiian themes of aloha and ohana (family) that run through the whole Lilo & Stitch series.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/leroy-stitch","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":650,"t":"Coraline","y":"2009","la":"9+","ca":"9+","full":"\"Cool but creepy animated fantasy too scary for young kids.\" CSM warns of a genuinely dark, unsettling tone, including implied child deaths, a severed hand that attacks the heroine, a strangling scene, and mild insults like \"jerkwad.\" Positive: Coraline's bravery and resourcefulness in facing the Other Mother, and her parents' humility in learning from her, model self-control, gratitude, and appreciating imperfect real life.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/coraline","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Horror"],"studio":"Focus Features","source":"CSM"},{"num":651,"t":"Dr. Dolittle: Million Dollar Mutts","y":"2009","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Latest sequel is surprisingly fun; good role models, too.\" A Hollywood agent gets comically chased by a menagerie of animals seeking justice, a sick dog recovers, there's one kiss between college-aged characters, and some brand product placement appears during a shopping scene. Positive: Maya Dolittle learns that shortcuts and celebrity promises can't replace real effort, owns up to her mistakes, and models a healthy body image and self-respect.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/dr-dolittle-million-dollar-mutts","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"20th Century Fox","source":"CSM"},{"num":652,"t":"Despicable Me","y":"2010","la":"5+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Clever, funny, and sweet villain-with-a-heart-of-gold tale.\" Supervillain Gru's schemes involve high-tech weapons blasting things to smithereens and scenes implying injury, though nobody is seriously hurt, and the orphanage keeps unwanted kids in a \"box of shame.\" Mild language includes \"stupid\" and \"poop.\" Positive: the three girls Gru adopts turn out to be the story's real role models, sweet and generous even as Gru's own heart softens, and the film's message that everyone -- even someone who seems irredeemable -- deserves love lands without being preachy.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/despicable-me","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Illumination","source":"CSM"},{"num":653,"t":"Arthur Christmas","y":"2011","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Fast-paced, funny holiday movie about the Christmas spirit.\" Concerns: a homeowner fires a gun at the sleigh, missile launches and other perilous chases, a tipsy Grandsanta and passing references to whiskey and pills, and some name-calling (\"bag of fleas,\" \"moron\") alongside outdated sexist and stereotyped jokes played for laughs. Positive: \"one person can really make a difference\" -- Arthur's compassion and perseverance save Christmas, and Mrs. Claus proves more capable than the men around her give her credit for.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/arthur-christmas","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Holiday"],"studio":"Sony Pictures Animation","source":"CSM"},{"num":654,"t":"Dolphin Tale","y":"2011","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"\"Heartwarming tale is sweet, inspiring for kids and tweens,\" per CSM, but the true story of rescue dolphin Winter opens with her rope-tangled, bloody tail found on a beach, and a wounded veteran returns home in a wheelchair before a hurricane later threatens the aquarium. Language stays mild (\"crappy,\" \"stupid\"). Positive: Sawyer's loyalty and Hazel's compassion drive an ensemble that shows teamwork saving a life, and Winter's prosthetic-tail journey models resilience and living with disability.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/dolphin-tale","genre":["Live-Action","Drama","Adventure"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"CSM"},{"num":655,"t":"Ernest & Celestine","y":"2012","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Touching animated story celebrates unlikely friendships.\" A gruff bear and a small orphaned mouse form a forbidden bond that defies their two prejudiced, segregated societies, leading to traps, a police chase, and a fire that puts both in real danger. Mild insults like \"moron\" and \"stupid\" turn up, along with scenes of stealing and breaking rules. Positive: Ernest and Celestine protect each other and prove their communities' fears about \"the other\" wrong, modeling loyalty and courage in defending a friendship.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ernest-celestine","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"StudioCanal","source":"CSM"},{"num":656,"t":"Despicable Me 2","y":"2013","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"More minions and romance make for a sweet, silly sequel.\" Concerns: cartoonish weapons (tasers, flame guns, razors) and minions who briefly transform into monstrous creatures, mild insults (\"nut job\"), a bit of toilet humor, and a blossoming romance between Gru and Lucy that includes a date and kisses. Positive: the strong father-daughter bond between Gru and his girls, plus teamwork and compassion as Gru trades villainy for protecting his family.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/despicable-me-2","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Illumination","source":"CSM"},{"num":657,"t":"Song of the Sea","y":"2014","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"\"Beautiful Irish tale explores myths, sibling relationship.\" CSM notes the film implies the mother's death in childbirth and features the menacing owl-witch Macha pursuing the children, alongside a few intense, heartbreaking moments. Positive: the story champions sibling love and cooperation, courage, and honestly embracing your feelings, with young Ben's growing bravery and the loyal dog Cu's heroics standing out as role models.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/song-of-the-sea","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"GKIDS","source":"CSM"},{"num":658,"t":"An American Girl: Grace Stirs Up Success","y":"2015","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Tale of young baker jam-packed with positive messages.\" CSM finds essentially no violence, language, romance, or substance concerns in this live-action American Girl story about a determined young baker navigating a trip to Paris and a newly blended family. Positive: discipline, follow-through, teamwork, and taking criticism constructively, with parents and grandparents offering strong mentoring throughout.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/an-american-girl-grace-stirs-up-success","genre":["Live-Action","Comedy","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"Other","source":"CSM"},{"num":659,"t":"Cinderella (2015)","y":"2015","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Visually impressive fairy tale promotes kindness.\" This live-action retelling stays close to the animated classic, opening with Ella's mother's drawn-out death and later her father's, both filled with real grief that may hit younger kids harder than the cartoon version. The stepmother's cruelty includes ripping Ella's dress and locking her in the attic, and mild insults like \"wretch\" and \"Cinderwench\" appear. Positive: Ella lives by her mother's advice to \"have courage and be kind,\" treating even her tormentors with compassion and respect.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/cinderella-2015","genre":["Live-Action","Fantasy","Romance"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":660,"t":"Albert","y":"2016","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Spunky tree's adventures are filled with festive messages.\" CSM flags a few dicey scenes, including a near-miss at a paper mill, plus mild bathroom-style language like \"butt\" and \"jerkweed.\" Positive: Albert's determination to be chosen as the city's Christmas tree despite others' doubts models self-confidence and optimism, while his friendship with Maisy reinforces loyalty and the idea that sharing joy brings it back.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/albert","genre":["Animation","Holiday","Comedy"],"studio":"Nickelodeon","source":"CSM"},{"num":661,"t":"Elena and the Secret of Avalor","y":"2016","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"CSM's tagline: \"Princess crossover features two strong female role models.\" A sorceress traps Elena's family inside inanimate objects, there are a few tense standoffs, and the villain takes a fall from a height whose outcome is briefly left uncertain. Positive: the story shows freedom and peace winning out over intimidation and fear, with Sofia coming across as clever, strong-minded, and brave, and Elena as resourceful and devoted to her family and kingdom, while the Avalor setting introduces kids to Latino dress, music, dance, and architecture.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/elena-and-the-secret-of-avalor","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":662,"t":"Ferdinand","y":"2017","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Sweet story about compassion, nonviolence has a few scares.\" Ferdinand is slashed with a spear during a tense bullfighting climax, there's a genuinely scary sequence in a meat-processing facility full of dangerous machinery, and bullies taunt him with names like \"flower boy\" and \"loser.\" Positive: Ferdinand's quiet refusal to fight, even when the whole world expects otherwise, models gentle strength and staying true to yourself, and the film never asks other characters to become like him -- just to accept him as he is.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ferdinand","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Blue Sky Studios","source":"CSM"},{"num":663,"t":"Dr. Seuss' The Grinch","y":"2018","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Pratfall-heavy take on holiday tale is fun, mostly gentle.\" Benedict Cumberbatch voices the Grinch, who schemes to steal Christmas from the too-cheerful residents of Whoville, leading to lots of physical comedy, sledding chaos, and a tense scene with the Grinch in peril on a mountain. Mild language (\"dumb,\" \"stupid,\" \"oh, sugar plum\") and a brief glimpse of the Grinch in his underwear round out the concerns. Positive: Cindy-Lou Who's generosity toward her hardworking mom and the Grinch's own transformation show how one act of kindness can change a hardened heart.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/dr-seuss-the-grinch","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Holiday"],"studio":"Illumination","source":"CSM"},{"num":664,"t":"Early Man","y":"2018","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"A charming stop-motion underdog sports comedy from Aardman, where Stone Age hero Dug rallies his tribe to save their home from invading Bronze Age conquerors. Expect slapstick violence (falls, hits, a village torn down), mild language like \"crap\" and \"stupid,\" some near-nakedness played for laughs, and potty humor. Positive: the film celebrates teamwork and perseverance, with strong female athletes standing up to bullies alongside Dug.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/early-man","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Sports"],"studio":"Aardman","source":"CSM"},{"num":665,"t":"A Shaun the Sheep Movie: Farmageddon","y":"2019","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Delightful family-friendly sequel has positive themes.\" CSM notes slapstick mayhem, mild peril as a villain hunts the alien Lu-La with a robotic rig, and a character hyped up on soda, all played for laughs with no lasting harm. Positive: the film's core theme of friendship across differences shines through Shaun and Lu-La's bond, with the whole flock demonstrating teamwork, empathy, and inclusiveness.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/a-shaun-the-sheep-movie-farmageddon","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Aardman","source":"CSM"},{"num":666,"t":"Frozen 2","y":"2019","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Slightly darker sequel charms with catchy songs, messages,\" says CSM. The story leans into battle flashbacks with swords, an enormous rock-giant chase, and a scene where a major character appears to meet a grim fate before it's undone; language is virtually nonexistent. Positive: Anna and Elsa lead through courage and mutual support rather than romance, and the film gently pushes kids to reckon with a colonized people's history and the cost of broken promises.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/frozen-2","genre":["Animation","Musical","Fantasy"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":667,"t":"Ella Bella Bingo","y":"2020","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Animated tale has engaging story and music, some peril.\" This Norwegian import follows spirited Ella Bella Bingo as jealousy over her best friend Henry's new pal threatens their bond. Cartoon peril includes a bull chase, a wild roller-coaster ride, and a spooky haunted-house scene with ghosts and skeletons, plus lots of dog-fart humor. Positive: the film models kindness, loyalty, and welcoming others, with Ella growing past possessiveness and the adults around her showing generosity and dependability.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/ella-bella-bingo","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Musical"],"studio":"Studio 100","source":"CSM"},{"num":668,"t":"Phineas and Ferb the Movie: Candace Against the Universe","y":"2020","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Cosmic adventure offers more franchise fun for families,\" per CSM. When Candace is whisked to an alien planet and hailed as \"The Chosen One,\" the action includes mind-controlling spores, kids getting tossed around during a crash-landing, and an alien abduction, alongside mild language like \"freak\" -- typical energetic Phineas and Ferb chaos rather than real menace. Positive: the film leans on teamwork and courage, with Candace working through guilt toward her brothers and even the villain learning a lesson about jealousy and self-worth.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/phineas-and-ferb-the-movie-candace-against-the-universe","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Sci-Fi"],"studio":"Disney","source":"CSM"},{"num":669,"t":"My Little Pony: A New Generation","y":"2021","la":"6+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Sweet tale of acceptance has some mildly scary scenes,\" per CSM. Earth ponies, unicorns, and pegasi distrust one another, and the peril -- ponies nearly falling from cliffs and buildings, a menacing military-style robot chasing defenseless characters -- can startle very young kids even though language stays at the level of \"stupid\" and \"brute.\" Positive: Sunny's courage in rejecting stereotypes and building bridges between the pony tribes carries a strong message about choosing friendship over fear.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/my-little-pony-a-new-generation","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Musical"],"studio":"Netflix","source":"CSM"},{"num":670,"t":"Epic Tails","y":"2022","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Mythology adventure has smart, brave characters; icky jokes.\" Set in ancient Greece, young mouse Pattie assembles a ragtag crew to recover a legendary treasure after a jealous Poseidon steals the golden fleece protecting her city. There's mild menace from a kraken and hungry cyclopes, name-calling like \"nerd\" and \"pipsqueak,\" and gross-out humor including vomiting and an exposed cyclops backside. Positive: Pattie leads with courage and resourcefulness, and the story champions teamwork and looking past assumptions about who can be a hero.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/epic-tails","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"TAT Productions","source":"CSM"},{"num":671,"t":"Luck","y":"2022","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Colorful, charming animated tale has positive messages.\" Sam, an unlucky orphan aging out of foster care, faces real disappointment as she's repeatedly passed over for adoption, and the Land of Bad Luck is populated by scary-looking monsters (though no one actually gets hurt). Mild language includes \"poop\" and \"screw it up.\" Positive: the film's core message that \"family is who you make it\" comes through Sam's resilience and gratitude, and both the Captain and the Dragon offer confident female leadership rare in animated fantasy.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/luck","genre":["Animation","Fantasy","Adventure"],"studio":"Skydance Animation","source":"CSM"},{"num":672,"t":"Marcel the Shell with Shoes On","y":"2022","la":"8+","ca":"8+","full":"\"Sweet, poignant mockumentary goes small to make big points.\" CSM flags everyday perils like falls and a cracked-shell injury to Nana Connie, a few mild swears such as \"idiot\" and \"oh my God,\" and some crude humor. Positive: Marcel's bravery, loyalty, and perseverance while searching for his lost family, plus Nana Connie's independent, risk-taking spirit, offer real lessons about community and doing the hard thing anyway.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/marcel-the-shell-with-shoes-on","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Coming-of-Age"],"studio":"A24","source":"CSM"},{"num":673,"t":"Flow","y":"2024","la":"7+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Superb animated 'cat-astrophe' has animals in peril.\" This dialogue-free Oscar winner follows a solitary cat and a growing crew of other animals surviving a catastrophic flood that submerges their world. Expect sustained life-or-death tension, a stampede, animal fights, near-drowning, and one moment that may suggest a character's death, all without any words to soften the mood. Positive: the animals learn to set aside their differences and cooperate, with a secretary bird sacrificing its own safety to protect the cat, modeling loyalty and teamwork.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/flow","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Drama"],"studio":"Dream Well Studio","source":"CSM"},{"num":674,"t":"David","y":"2025","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Moving animated Biblical musical has battle violence, peril.\" This faith-based animated musical follows David from shepherd boy to king, with non-graphic but thematic battle violence -- including the fight with Goliath, swords and spears in play -- that may frighten younger viewers, plus very mild language (\"heck,\" \"wretched\"). Positive: courage in faith, humility, and perseverance, with David portrayed as a great but flawed hero who leads with kindness and loyalty.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/david","genre":["Animation","Musical","Drama"],"studio":"Other","source":"CSM"},{"num":675,"t":"The Last Whale Singer","y":"2025","la":"7+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Fact-filled animated whale tale deals with loss; some peril,\" per CSM. Orphaned humpback Vincent watches his parents killed by a ship in a frightening early scene, later discovering the euphemistic \"star pool\" is actually a bone-filled graveyard, and a tentacled sea monster hunts him with poison and a jump-scare shark encounter along the way. Positive: Vincent's courage in forging his own path rather than his family's legacy, nanny Walter's steady care, and deaf orca Darya's heightened senses (framed as a strength, not a limitation) carry real messages about perseverance and difference.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/the-last-whale-singer","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Fantasy"],"studio":"Telescope Animation","source":"CSM"},{"num":676,"t":"Coyote vs. Acme","y":"2026","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"\"Great messages in chaotic, hilarious, touching 'toon mashup,\" says CSM of this live-action/animation hybrid, where Wile E. Coyote sues ACME with the help of a struggling lawyer. Slapstick violence is constant (characters flattened, slimed, hit by trucks) alongside a knife threat, explosions, and a train crash played for cartoon laughs, plus scattered mild profanity and a joke about a bottle labeled \"XXX.\" Positive: the story champions perseverance over giving up, and Wile E. and Kevin's unlikely friendship models loyalty and standing up to a callous corporation.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/coyote-vs-acme","genre":["Live-Action","Animation","Comedy"],"studio":"Warner Bros.","source":"CSM"},{"num":677,"t":"Minions & Monsters","y":"2026","la":"7+","ca":"7+","full":"\"Homage to Hollywood mixes massive monsters, 'toon violence,\" per CSM, this Hollywood-set adventure has giant monsters smashing property, a shark attacking the crew's boat, villains dispatched (including one beheaded by an ax), and a single \"handsome bastard\" line amid otherwise mild language; adults toast champagne and cocktails at studio parties. Positive: the Minions and their friends pursue creative dreams and stick together through loyalty and teamwork, though their habit of admiring villains and causing chaos means they're better comic relief than role models.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/minions-monsters","genre":["Animation","Comedy","Adventure"],"studio":"Illumination","source":"CSM"},{"num":678,"t":"PAW Patrol: The Dino Movie","y":"2026","la":"6+","ca":"6+","full":"\"Canine heroes meet dinos in engaging but perilous adventure,\" per CSM, the pups' third big-screen outing turns up the intensity with river rapids, a dinosaur stampede, an erupting volcano throwing lava and fireballs, and a nighttime search for a missing friend that plays genuinely scary. Language is limited to words like \"stinky\" and \"nasty.\" Positive: Marshall learns to stop beating himself up over clumsiness, Ryder leads with encouragement, and new pup Rex shows that physical differences don't limit resourcefulness.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/paw-patrol-the-dino-movie","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Paramount","source":"CSM"},{"num":679,"t":"Toy Story 5","y":"2026","la":"5+","ca":"5+","full":"\"Some peril in thoughtful kids-and-tech animated adventure.\" As Bonnie grows attached to a tablet named Lilypad, the toys attempt rescues and daring missions with real tension; mild language (\"shut up,\" \"darned\") and a low-battery gag where tech toys slur their speech like they're intoxicated round out the concerns. Positive: Jessie models courage, leadership, and teamwork, and the film opens up conversation about balancing screen time with imaginative play.","srcUrl":"https://www.commonsensemedia.org/movie-reviews/toy-story-5","genre":["Animation","Adventure","Comedy"],"studio":"Pixar","source":"CSM"}];
